\section{Fits from \code{engine = "julia"}}{


A fit produced by the DRM.jl bridge (\code{engine = "julia"}, class
\code{drmTMB_julia}) dispatches through this same generic and returns the same
four-column \code{drm_check} schema, but it reports a deliberately \strong{different
and much shorter} set of rows. Almost every check listed above reads a
\code{TMB} object or a \code{\link[TMB:sdreport]{TMB::sdreport()}} that a bridge fit does not have, so a
clean \code{engine = "julia"} table is a narrower claim than a clean
\code{engine = "tmb"} table on the same model. The rows are:

\describe{
\item{\code{engine_route}}{Always a \code{note}. Names the engine, the DRM.jl
route, and the estimator (\code{"engine=julia; route=<route>; estimator=<ML|REML>"}), and its message lists the native
\code{engine = "tmb"} checks that did not run. Being a \code{note}, it never
flips \code{attr(x, "ok")}: reporting which machinery ran is not itself a
fault.}
\item{\code{optimizer_convergence}}{Whether DRM.jl reported convergence. This
is DRM.jl's own convergence flag, not \code{nlminb()}'s \code{convergence} code,
and the two are not claimed to agree.}
\item{\code{fixed_gradient}}{Route-aware. Only some DRM.jl routes store a
gradient callback, and the bridge omits the gradient entirely rather
than sending a fabricated one on a route without it; on such a route
this row is a \code{note} naming the route, stationarity is \strong{not} scored,
and \code{attr(x, "ok")} does not reflect it. When a gradient is present the
row reports \verb{max|gradient|} and the largest component. In both cases the
value string carries \verb{source=}, naming which producer made the number in
DRM.jl's own \code{grad_source} vocabulary (\code{"locscale"}, \code{"stored"},
\code{"forward"}, \code{"finite"}, \code{"none"}, \code{"unavailable"}) so that an exact
analytic gradient, an automatic-differentiation gradient, and a finite
difference accurate only to roughly \code{1e-6} relative do not print
identically. The current bridge payload has exactly one gradient
producer, the fit's own stored callback, so a gradient that crossed the
bridge is reported as \code{source=stored}. When no gradient crossed, the
bridge does not report which of the remaining five cases applies, so the
row records \code{source=unknown} -- deliberately not one of DRM.jl's six
values -- and points you at DRM.jl's own \code{check_drm(fit)} in Julia,
whose \code{grad_source} field tells them apart. This is not TMB's
\code{sdreport()} gradient and the two engines are not claimed to agree
numerically. A source outside that vocabulary, or one that contradicts
the payload (a producer named when no gradient crossed, or \code{"none"} /
\code{"unavailable"} alongside one that did), aborts rather than being
printed.}
\item{\code{bridge_covariance}}{The DRM.jl analogue of \code{sdreport_status}:
whether the fixed-effect covariance the bridge marshalled back is
complete (\code{ok}), partial (some distributional parameters finite, others
not, which some structured routes produce by design), or unavailable.
\code{\link[TMB:sdreport]{TMB::sdreport()}} is not run for a bridge fit.}
\item{\code{bridge_standard_errors}}{The DRM.jl analogue of
\code{standard_errors_finite}: whether every fixed-effect standard error
implied by that covariance is a finite real number. A complete
covariance can still carry a negative variance on the diagonal, which
\code{bridge_covariance} cannot see.}
}

The \code{gradient_tolerance} argument applies as it does for a native fit; the
\code{rho_boundary} and \code{sd_boundary} arguments do not, because the
corresponding rows are not computed for a bridge fit.
}

